<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Demetae</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 75 CE (approx.) · capital Moridunum (Carmarthen)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of south-west Wales whose name survives in Dyfed, and who accepted Roman rule with little recorded fighting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Demetae lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, a region of small defended farmsteads, known as raths, and coastal promontory forts. Ptolemy recorded them in the 2nd century, but no earlier writer describes them, and they were probably a grouping of local communities with long roots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike their neighbours the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/silures">Silures</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a>, the Demetae are not recorded as resisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and their territory needed few garrisons. Farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-rearing sustained a dispersed population, and the region looked west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> mined gold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolaucothi-gold-mines">Dolaucothi</a> on the eastern edge of their land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 70s the Demetae formed a civitas centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Moridunum</a>, modern Carmarthen, one of the westernmost towns of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>. The name passed to the post-<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dyfed">Dyfed</a>, ruled by a dynasty of Irish descent, and survives in the modern county of Dyfed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/demetae.jpg" alt="Demetae" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: author of source image, plus my additions (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wales.pre-Roman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 75 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Moridunum (Carmarthen)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
